<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.StaticWebAssets">

  <!--
    The browser host on the MONO WebAssembly runtime: the third sibling of CupriFace.Shell
    (desktop) and CupriFace.Android (phone). An app author writes `WebHost.Run(new MyApp())` and
    gets the canvas, the frame loop, input, the ARIA mirror, the IME, the clipboard and
    browser-decoded video — the same host the Showcase runs, rather than a copy of it.

    Same layering law as the other two: the ENGINE has no idea the browser exists. Everything
    browser lives here.

    WHY THE NAME SAYS Mono. There are two web hosts, and the runtime is the choice between them:
    this one runs on the wasm runtime that ships with the .NET SDK, so it builds wherever
    `dotnet publish` does; the NativeAOT-LLVM host (samples/WebLlvm, packaged by #78) compiles the
    engine properly and is several times faster, at the cost of an experimental toolchain from the
    dotnet-experimental feed. Neither is simply better, so neither gets to be `CupriFace.Web` —
    that name is deliberately unclaimed rather than quietly meaning whichever host we favoured on
    the day. A package ID that silently changes runtime under consumers is a worse outcome than a
    longer name.

    The API is identical across both, on purpose: namespace CupriFace.Web, type WebHost, method
    Run. Switching hosts is a PackageReference change and no app code, which is what makes "the
    other one is the fallback" true rather than aspirational.

    Why this package exists at all: `samples/WebWasm` used to BE the web host, so a second web app
    had to copy ~1,000 lines of Program.cs + main.js + video backend, an AOT workaround for a bug
    in CupriFace's own code, and two font files whose absence renders every app monospaced with no
    error. The copies silently lost the ARIA mirror, IME positioning, touch and video, because
    hand-carrying 700 lines to get a first render is not a trade anyone makes deliberately (#73).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <!-- The straight-alpha conversion writes into the staging bitmap through a raw pointer, and
         Present hands JS a Span over WASM memory rather than allocating 2.7 MB per frame. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <!-- Browser-only by definition; the JSExport/JSImport platform hints (CA1416 "only supported
         on browser") are expected here and are not actionable. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1416</NoWarn>
    <!-- Microsoft.NET.Sdk.StaticWebAssets is the SDK whose entire job is the one thing this
         package needs: the `_content/<PackageId>/` protocol, which is how main.js reaches a
         consuming app through a ProjectReference and a PackageReference alike, lands in the right
         place in its publish output, and gets its .br/.gz siblings and cache headers.

         Not the Razor SDK. That was the first thing that worked, and it works, but it is the Razor
         SDK plus two switches to turn Razor off — for a package with no Razor in it. This is the
         same feature without the detour, and nothing Blazor or ASP.NET Core comes with it: the
         built assembly references only System.*, SkiaSharp and CupriFace, the nuspec declares no
         frameworkReference, and the published wasm app ships no ASP.NET Core or Blazor assembly.
         (The two Microsoft.AspNetCore.StaticWebAssets*.props files inside the nupkg are build-time
         item metadata for main.js — a path, a base path and cache headers. Nothing runs.) -->
    <StaticWebAssetBasePath>_content/CupriFace.Web.Mono</StaticWebAssetBasePath>
    <!-- The namespace stays CupriFace.Web even though the assembly is CupriFace.Web.Mono: an app
         writes the same `WebHost.Run(new MyApp())` against either host. -->
    <RootNamespace>CupriFace.Web</RootNamespace>
    <Description>Run a CupriApp in the browser, on a canvas — no Blazor, no WebView, no JavaScript to write. Your whole Program.cs is WebHost.Run(new MyApp()); the package brings the frame loop, pointer/touch/wheel/keyboard input, the touch gesture recognizer, an ARIA mirror so screen readers can read the canvas, IME composition, the clipboard and browser-decoded video.

This is the MONO runtime host — the compatible one. It builds with the wasm runtime that ships in the .NET SDK, so it works wherever `dotnet publish` does. The trade-off is speed: a Mono AOT codegen defect forces the engine to run interpreted, roughly 8x slower than native, so heavy interaction is noticeably less smooth.

If you want the fast one, CupriFace.Web.NativeAot compiles the engine properly, at the cost of an experimental (dotnet/runtimelab) toolchain. Both packages expose exactly the same WebHost.Run API, so choosing between them — or falling back — is a PackageReference change and no app code.</Description>
    <PackageTags>cupriface;ui;webassembly;wasm;browser;canvas;skia;html;css;mono</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CupriFace\CupriFace.csproj" />
    <!-- Skia + HarfBuzz natives for the browser (the engine's desktop natives are ignored here).
         BOTH are required and both fail LATE if missing, which is exactly why they belong to the
         package rather than to every app: text shaping (FontService → SKShaper) dlopens
         libHarfBuzzSharp at the first text draw, so an app without the wasm asset boots happily
         and then dies rendering its first glyph. Keep versions in sync with the engine's SkiaSharp
         (src/SkiaNativeAssets.props documents the pins). -->
    <PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp" Version="3.116.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.WebAssembly" Version="3.116.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="HarfBuzzSharp.NativeAssets.WebAssembly" Version="8.3.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The sans faces both web hosts embed, and the guard that they actually got embedded. Shared
       rather than owned: neither host owns them (see src/WebFonts.props). Registered at Init, where
       an app cannot forget them. -->
  <Import Project="..\WebFonts.props" />

  <!-- The host itself, shared with CupriFace.Web.NativeAot as source (#79). -->
  <Import Project="..\WebCore.props" />

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- OFL-licensed; the licence travels in the package that ships the bytes. -->
    <None Include="..\WebFonts\OFL.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES\fonts-OFL.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- main.js is the JS half of the host and ships WITH it, so the two halves cannot drift: every
         module name it binds is a [JSImport] in this assembly. wwwroot/ is picked up as static web
         assets by the SDK above, so a consuming app serves it at _content/CupriFace.Web/main.js and
         writes no JS of its own.

         The default page is NOT a static web asset: the WebAssembly SDK requires the app to own
         wwwroot/index.html, and two index.html files claiming the same placeholder target path is a
         hard build error. It ships as a template to copy instead, which is also the honest shape —
         the page shell (title, background, how the canvas is sized) is the one genuinely
         app-specific piece of a web host. -->
    <None Include="template\index.html" Pack="true" PackagePath="template\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Reaches nupkg CONSUMERS only — buildTransitive does not cross ProjectReference edges, so
         repo-internal apps (samples/WebWasm) set the same properties in their own csproj. -->
    <None Include="buildTransitive\CupriFace.Web.Mono.props" Pack="true" PackagePath="buildTransitive\" />
    <None Include="buildTransitive\CupriFace.Web.Mono.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="buildTransitive\" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
